00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:20,560
&gt;&gt; Hello and welcome to Software

9
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:22,550
Engineering Radio. This is your host

10
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:23,870
Arun Ponnusamy.

11
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:27,030
Today I'm joined by Will Wilson, CEO and

12
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:30,510
co-founder of Antithesis. Will started

13
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:32,990
off in biotech and went on to work on

14
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,950
distributed systems at FoundationDB,

15
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:36,510
Apple, and Google.

16
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:38,550
And then he eventually went on to found

17
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:40,550
Antithesis, an autonomous testing

18
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:42,590
platform based on deterministic

19
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,550
simulation testing.

20
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:45,870
Will, welcome to the show.

21
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:47,510
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for having me.

22
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:49,550
&gt;&gt; For our listeners who may not be

23
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:51,110
familiar with the concept, could you

24
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:53,670
share what is DST or deterministic

25
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:55,150
simulation testing?

26
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:57,350
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure. So, let's start with the way

27
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:59,270
that people normally test software,

28
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:00,470
right? So, the way they normally test

29
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:02,590
software is first you write some code,

30
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:04,270
and then you try and think about what

31
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:06,590
situations you want to cover with a

32
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:08,470
test, what situations you want to make

33
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:10,710
sure are going to work in real life, and

34
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:12,390
then you write some tests to cover those

35
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,350
situations, right? And then you

36
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:15,750
occasionally run those tests, maybe you

37
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:17,630
do them on every CI build, or maybe you

38
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:19,270
do them on every PR, maybe you do them

39
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:20,350
only when you're about to release your

40
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:21,630
software,

41
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:25,510
but they cover those cases, and you hope

42
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:27,150
that when you deploy to production,

43
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:29,110
everything is going to go okay. Now, let

44
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:31,630
me ask you a question. Do you believe if

45
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:33,630
all your tests pass, that everything is

46
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:34,870
going to be okay?

47
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:36,830
&gt;&gt; I would like to think so.

48
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:38,750
&gt;&gt; But do you actually?

49
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:40,910
&gt;&gt; Uh well, if anything last 20 years have

50
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:42,350
shown me, no.

51
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:44,950
&gt;&gt; Right, right. Exactly, so why is that?

52
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:48,150
Well, it's because we, I just said,

53
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:50,470
you're writing the tests that cover the

54
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:51,950
cases that you thought were important to

55
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:53,870
cover. But the things that are actually

56
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:55,910
going to screw you in production are the

57
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:57,990
situations that you did not think of

58
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,270
ahead of time. They're the things you

59
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:01,390
did not think to cover, right? And so

60
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:02,710
what that means is this conventional

61
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:05,310
strategy to writing tests is very very

62
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:07,390
good for catching regressions, right? If

63
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:08,469
there's something that I know my

64
00:02:08,479 --> 00:02:10,669
software does correctly and I

65
00:02:10,679 --> 00:02:12,910
accidentally break it in a new version,

66
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:14,390
if I have a test for it, that's a pretty

67
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:16,230
good at catching that. But a test is

68
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:19,270
very bad at finding out if my software

69
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:21,630
actually works in the real world in all

70
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:23,470
the unanticipated or unexpected

71
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:25,590
situations that the real world will

72
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:27,030
throw at me.

73
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:29,790
So, deterministic simulation testing is

74
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:31,990
a very different strategy, which tries

75
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,470
to actually give us the confidence that

76
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:36,310
if our test pass,

77
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:38,390
things will go well in the real world.

78
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:40,630
And the way it does that is basically by

79
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:43,790
constructing a model of the real world,

80
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:46,190
a whole system simulation

81
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:49,430
where random requests come in and ask

82
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:51,750
your software to do things and tasks are

83
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:54,230
going to run in random orders and these

84
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:55,590
requests are going to have slightly

85
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:58,230
randomized parameters. And also the

86
00:02:58,240 --> 00:02:59,949
things that happen in the real world

87
00:02:59,959 --> 00:03:01,949
that can go wrong are also going to

88
00:03:01,959 --> 00:03:04,030
happen, right? So, occasionally requests

89
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:05,750
will fail. Requests to downstream

90
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:07,750
dependent systems will fail. Network

91
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:09,750
requests may get delayed or reordered.

92
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:12,430
Threads may randomly pause and run

93
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:14,110
later. The garbage collector may just

94
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:15,790
turn on and that whole node may slow

95
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,350
down, right? All the stuff that causes

96
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:19,590
people to get paged in the middle of the

97
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:21,990
night and causes weird production

98
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:23,830
behaviors that nobody can understand,

99
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:25,310
we're going to do all that, too. And

100
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:26,670
we're going to do it at a vastly

101
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:29,870
accelerated rate so that we cover many

102
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:32,310
many years or centuries worth of real

103
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:35,229
world badness in just a few seconds or

104
00:03:35,239 --> 00:03:37,949
minutes in our tests. And if our

105
00:03:37,959 --> 00:03:39,710
software survives all that and things

106
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:41,990
actually go okay, we're now going to

107
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,790
have the confidence to actually deploy

108
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:45,190
and to actually feel like things will be

109
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,750
all right when we have it in the real

110
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:47,830
world.

111
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:49,190
The

112
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:51,870
final ingredient to this, the part that

113
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:54,390
makes it deterministic

114
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:57,710
is our model of the real world, unlike

115
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:00,030
the real world, is perfectly

116
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:03,070
deterministic. Meaning it's all driven

117
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:04,870
from a random number generator that's

118
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:07,670
under our control, right? So, this is

119
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:09,949
very important because sometimes what

120
00:04:09,959 --> 00:04:13,190
happens is there's a particular

121
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:15,070
Let's say that a packet takes 21

122
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:16,590
milliseconds to get from service A to

123
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:19,110
service B. And if it takes 19

124
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:20,990
milliseconds instead, something goes

125
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:22,270
wrong, right? There's some distributed

126
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:23,790
race condition. Something something

127
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:25,470
loses and as a result there's some bug

128
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:27,150
that a user can see.

129
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:28,590
You may have great difficulty

130
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:30,710
replicating that situation again, right?

131
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:32,710
Maybe it only happened it only took that

132
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:34,350
long because of some very rare set of

133
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:35,750
circumstances.

134
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:38,550
But the determinism of DST means that

135
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:40,830
any set of circumstances, no matter how

136
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:42,870
rare, no matter how unusual, we can

137
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:45,110
perfectly replicate. And so if we see a

138
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:47,790
bug once, we can see it as many times as

139
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:49,990
we want and we can debug it with very

140
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:51,750
very high efficiency.

141
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:53,990
How you actually achieve that is sort of

142
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,230
the hard part. But once you have it,

143
00:04:56,240 --> 00:04:58,710
it's an incredible asset.

144
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:00,470
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you. Before I dive in, I

145
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:02,990
would like to get some of the origin

146
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,390
stories here. I hear it started off in

147
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:07,070
FoundationDB. Could you share some of

148
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:08,310
that background there?

149
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:09,750
&gt;&gt; Sure, yeah. So, this is sort of a fun

150
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:12,550
story. Basically, actually even before

151
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:14,630
FoundationDB, my co-founder Dave

152
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:16,670
Scherer, before FoundationDB, had

153
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:19,230
another company called Visual Sciences,

154
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:20,950
which you can think of as having made a

155
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:24,270
very very early web analytics product

156
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:26,630
that was backed by a very powerful

157
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:29,390
analytic database that's sort of a very

158
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:31,790
early version of Google BigQuery, right?

159
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:33,190
And this would have been in the early

160
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:35,670
2000s. So, it was really very very ahead

161
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:36,870
of its time.

162
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:39,230
So, when Dave was building that big

163
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:41,310
analytic data store, he was really

164
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,230
interested in using model-based

165
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:46,510
techniques to look for sort of long-tail

166
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:49,670
performance pathologies and like strange

167
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:52,510
sort of high latency corner cases.

168
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:55,030
And so he built this little like model

169
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:58,110
based simulation of the database

170
00:05:58,120 --> 00:05:59,830
to see if he could find these issues.

171
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:01,630
It's sort of like in some ways similar

172
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:03,110
to formal methods if you're familiar

173
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:04,270
with that, but he was using it to

174
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:05,550
analyze performance rather than

175
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:06,790
correctness.

176
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:08,750
The problem that Dave ran into was of

177
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:11,070
course once he had this all working in

178
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:12,470
the model

179
00:06:12,480 --> 00:06:14,190
how do you know that your real code is

180
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:16,470
behaving anything like the model, right?

181
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:18,630
And so that company got sold and and

182
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:20,870
that never went anywhere and but it

183
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,830
stuck around in the back of his mind.

184
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,390
And so then when we were doing

185
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:28,150
FoundationDB he had the same idea again

186
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:29,950
this time to use it to study correctness

187
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:31,470
rather than performance.

188
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:34,110
But then he had the further idea what if

189
00:06:34,120 --> 00:06:37,270
we just made the actual program into the

190
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:39,710
model, right? Then you would never have

191
00:06:39,720 --> 00:06:42,670
to worry that oh I did some analysis on

192
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:44,270
this model and it gave me this result,

193
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:46,190
but actually my real world code has a

194
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:48,110
bug in it so it doesn't perfectly match

195
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:49,790
that specification.

196
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:51,310
So the trouble is you know real world

197
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:53,470
code has things like concurrency and it

198
00:06:53,480 --> 00:06:55,510
has things like it reads the clock and

199
00:06:55,520 --> 00:06:56,870
it gets random numbers from the

200
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:58,830
operating system and it it does all

201
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:00,030
kinds of things that make it

202
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:02,510
fundamentally non-deterministic and make

203
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:04,270
it so that if you run some battery of

204
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:07,310
tests or conduct some simulation

205
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:09,830
the result might vary from time to time.

206
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:14,830
And so we set about writing FoundationDB

207
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:17,790
in a very special way so that it could

208
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:20,350
be perfectly simulated. We did things

209
00:07:20,360 --> 00:07:23,630
like we used our own user space

210
00:07:23,640 --> 00:07:26,430
concurrency primitives and scheduling so

211
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:28,430
that the kernel was never deciding which

212
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:30,990
task to run. It's like sort of similar

213
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,750
to the Node.js concurrency model, but we

214
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:36,310
were doing that in C++ long beforehand.

215
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:38,670
We obviously had to mock out or stub out

216
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:40,150
any form of network communication

217
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:42,430
between nodes and handle that in

218
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:45,470
process. We had a fake clock and a fake

219
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:46,830
random number generator that got

220
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:48,710
accessed instead of the ones provided by

221
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:51,710
the OS. And as we sort of knocked away

222
00:07:51,720 --> 00:07:53,350
each of these forms of randomness and

223
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:55,070
each of these forms of non-determinism,

224
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:57,870
the simulation became more controllable

225
00:07:57,880 --> 00:08:00,110
until we eventually got to a point where

226
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:01,870
it really was like you could really just

227
00:08:01,880 --> 00:08:04,550
run a program and it would simulate an

228
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:08,030
entire interacting FoundationDB cluster

229
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:10,110
with possibly dozens or hundreds of

230
00:08:10,120 --> 00:08:12,830
simulated nodes speaking over simulated

231
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:15,270
networks with, you know, randomly

232
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:17,190
introduced networking delays and

233
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:19,270
congestion behavior and whatever else

234
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:22,030
you want. And this was just such an

235
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:23,790
insanely

236
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:27,830
productive mode in which to work. Like

237
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:29,230
people who are familiar with

238
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:31,390
FoundationDB these days mostly know it

239
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:34,029
for its reputation, which is being one

240
00:08:34,039 --> 00:08:35,870
of the highest quality databases ever

241
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:38,469
made, never losing your data, never

242
00:08:38,479 --> 00:08:40,469
doing anything wrong. And it's true, we

243
00:08:40,479 --> 00:08:42,070
got that. And we got that because of

244
00:08:42,080 --> 00:08:44,790
this technique. But the thing that made

245
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:46,750
almost an even bigger difference was

246
00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:48,310
that we were able to get that with a

247
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:50,230
very, very small team.

248
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:52,950
Because what this technique enables is

249
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,750
like this incredible form

250
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:58,110
of pair programming with the computer

251
00:08:58,120 --> 00:08:59,829
where you write some code and then you

252
00:08:59,839 --> 00:09:02,150
run some simulations and you say, "Did I

253
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:04,510
introduce any bugs?" And, you know, if

254
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:06,710
the answer is yes, then like you just

255
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:09,030
try again. It's like having an oracle or

256
00:09:09,040 --> 00:09:10,590
a genie that can tell you if your code

257
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:12,110
is correct, right? It's like this

258
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:14,829
incredible, incredible accelerator for

259
00:09:14,839 --> 00:09:16,710
an entire team. And that was what made

260
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:18,430
us really excited about this technique

261
00:09:18,440 --> 00:09:20,590
and I think this is why so many others

262
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:22,190
have started adopting it and picking it

263
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:23,310
up as well.

264
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:24,430
&gt;&gt; Interesting.

265
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:26,630
That reminds me as you go deeper, right?

266
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:28,190
I mean

267
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:30,550
to really simulation, you need a model.

268
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:32,550
So, how do you avoid the paradox of hey

269
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:34,030
look, I don't know what I don't know and

270
00:09:34,040 --> 00:09:35,430
I build this model?

271
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:36,990
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that is a really, really good

272
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,030
question. And basically, the answer is

273
00:09:40,040 --> 00:09:42,030
you have to err a little bit on the side

274
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:43,830
